{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the variance of the lotteries to determine preference. Variance measures the spread of outcomes around the expected value and reflects risk. For Lottery A: outcomes are 6000 with 25% and 0 with 75%. For Lottery B: outcomes are 4000 with 25%, 2000 with 25%, and 0 with 50%. We can compute the variance for each lottery. Variance = Σ(probability × (outcome - expected value)²). First, compute expected values: EA = 0.25×6000 + 0.75×0 = 1500. EB = 0.25×4000 + 0.25×2000 + 0.5×0 = 1000 + 500 + 0 = 1500. Both lotteries have the same expected value (1500). Now compute variance: VA = 0.25×(6000−1500)² + 0.75×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(4500)² + 0.75×(1500)² = 0.25×20,250,000 + 0.75×2,250,000 = 5,062,500 + 1,687,500 = 6,750,000. VB = 0.25×(4000−1500)² + 0.25×(2000−1500)² + 0.5×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(2500)² + 0.25×(500)² + 0.5×(1500)² = 0.25×6,250,000 + 0.25×250,000 + 0.5×2,250,000 = 1,562,500 + 62,500 + 1,125,000 = 2,749,000. Thus, VA = 6,750,000 > VB = 2,749,000. Therefore, Lottery A has a larger variance. The decision reason prefers the lottery with the larger variance, so it would prefer Lottery A. The reason is applicable to the decision problem and can be evaluated using the outcomes and probabilities provided.",
